cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Crucible: a semantic screen for feature-level target leakage in tabular datasets"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
# Three fields below cannot be filled from the source tree and must be set
# before the Zenodo deposit: the author's family name, ORCID, and the public
# repository URL. Everything else here is final.
authors:
  - given-names: Mukilan
abstract: >-
  Crucible audits a tabular dataset for feature-level target leakage: columns
  whose value encodes the outcome they would be used to predict. A language
  model reads the column names, and optionally the dataset's own data
  dictionary, against a stated target and a stated prediction point, and judges
  each column several times in different column orders. A correlation screen
  runs beside it as a deliberately weaker second witness, and the disagreement
  between the two is the tool's main output. Nothing is deleted without human
  confirmation. The tool then fits the same learners on identical folds with an
  identical hyperparameter search across three arms, keeping every column,
  removing the confirmed ones, and removing instead whatever a correlation
  threshold would have removed, and reports what the leakage was worth and
  whether the cheap check would have found it.
keywords:
  - data leakage
  - target leakage
  - tabular machine learning
  - data quality
  - large language models
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/smukilan9-ship-it/Crucible-Leakage"
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-08-16"
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Leakage in Data Mining: Formulation, Detection, and Avoidance"
    authors:
      - family-names: Kaufman
        given-names: Shachar
      - family-names: Rosset
        given-names: Saharon
      - family-names: Perlich
        given-names: Claudia
    year: 2011
    collection-title: >-
      Proceedings of the 17th ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge
      Discovery and Data Mining
    start: 556
    end: 563
    notes: >-
      The formulation Crucible implements. Kaufman and colleagues set out the
      condition a feature must meet in terms of what was knowable at the moment
      of prediction; Crucible is an attempt to apply that condition to a table
      whose columns have to be read rather than computed over.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Leakage and the Reproducibility Crisis in Machine-Learning-Based Science
    authors:
      - family-names: Kapoor
        given-names: Sayash
      - family-names: Narayanan
        given-names: Arvind
    year: 2023
    journal: Patterns
